\section{Supplementary Figures}
\label{app:supp_figs}

These figures support the main text but are not needed to follow it.

\subsection{EFE Trajectory Decomposition}
\label{app:efe_trajectory}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_efe_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{EFE decomposition within Tiger episodes ($H{=}6$). The agent commits at the crossover where commit value (circles) exceeds observe value (squares). As belief entropy (dashed line with shading) decreases, the transition from exploration to exploitation occurs automatically.}
\label{fig:traj}
\Description{Three line plots for a 4-step, an 8-step, and a 10-step episode, each with a rising vermillion curve that starts near minus 45, a nearly flat blue curve near plus 5, and a green dashed entropy curve with light green fill plotted on a green secondary axis shown on every panel. A dotted vertical line marks the step where the vermillion curve crosses above the blue one. In the 4-step panel the entropy declines steadily and the crossing occurs at step 3. In the 8-step and 10-step panels the vermillion curve and the entropy oscillate up and down several times before the crossings at steps 7 and 9.}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:traj} decomposes EFE within Tiger episodes. The transition from exploration to commitment needs no separate stopping rule.

\subsection{Observation Action Scaling and Adaptive Test Selection}
\label{app:obs_scaling}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_obs_scaling.pdf}
\caption{Observation action scaling on Diagnosis ($N{=}8$, $H{=}3$, 500 episodes). EFE separates from Planning only once several tests compete. The gap is zero at $K{=}1$ (bit-identical policies), $-0.6$pp and within seed-level sampling error at $K{=}2$, and $+13.2$pp at $K{=}3$, where choosing among differentially informative tests is exactly what the information-gain term contributes. InfoGain-Tuned is not part of this battery, so the legend carries four agents rather than the five of Figure~\ref{fig:sweep}.}
\label{fig:obs_scaling}
\Description{Two line plots against the number of observation actions, from 1 to 3. In the left panel success rates rise for all four agents, from roughly 19 to 23 percent at one action to about 96 to 98 percent for the vermillion and pink lines, 83 percent for the blue line, and about 50 percent for the gray line at three actions. In the right panel mean reward rises from between about minus 40 and minus 43 at one action to between about minus 7 and minus 23 at three actions, with the vermillion line ending highest and the pink dotted line starting lowest.}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{figures/fig_extended_efe.pdf}
\caption{EFE decomposition over an extended Diagnosis episode ($N{=}8$, $K{=}3$). (a)~Value of committing vs.\ observing. The open circle marks the value crossover interpolated between sampled steps. The dotted vertical line marks the actual discrete commit step in all four panels. (b)~Per-test expected information gain. Tests become differentially informative as belief concentrates, and the agent shifts its test selection as they do, though the recursive score it optimizes is not the one-step information gain plotted here. (c)~Belief entropy decay. (d)~Test selection sequence showing the agent's adaptive strategy.}
\label{fig:extended_efe}
\Description{Four stacked panels sharing a time axis over roughly 30 steps, with a dotted vertical commit line spanning all four. In the top panel a solid black curve for the value of committing rises from about minus 43 with repeated pronounced dips, touching a smoother dashed gray curve for the value of observing several times before crossing it at an open circle interpolated between the final two sampled steps and identified in the legend. In the second panel three purple lines with distinct line styles and markers for Tests 0, 1, and 2 start near 0.19 bits. The two darker lines drop to about 0.03 bits at different times while the lightest line keeps oscillating between about 0.07 and 0.19 bits, staying at or above the other two throughout, and only drops to about 0.03 at the final step. In the third panel a dashed green entropy curve above a pale fill decays from 3 bits to under 1 bit with small bumps. The bottom strip gives each chosen test index in the same purple cell, with runs of the two darker tests early, then mostly the lightest test interrupted near the middle by two darker steps.}
\end{figure}

At fixed $N{=}8$, EFE and Planning are bit-identical at $K{=}1$, within seed-level sampling error at $K{=}2$ ($43.2\%$ vs.\ $43.8\%$), and separated at $K{=}3$ ($96.0\%$ vs.\ $82.8\%$). This is a threshold pattern, not steady growth with test count (Figure~\ref{fig:obs_scaling}). The main Diagnosis battery separately shows a gap with two tests. Figure~\ref{fig:extended_efe} illustrates adaptive selection as tests become differently informative, but the chosen test maximizes a recursive score and need not maximize panel (b)'s one-step information gain.

\subsection{Reward Asymmetry on Tiger}
\label{app:tiger_sweep}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_asymmetry_sweep.pdf}
\caption{Reward asymmetry sweep on Tiger ($H{=}6$, 500 episodes per point, 5 canonical seeds). EFE and Planning adapt their listening as the penalty grows, while the myopic InfoGain-Tuned agent at its fixed tuned weight listens $4.37$ times at every penalty. The accompanying text gives the per-agent observation counts and reward gaps.}
\label{fig:sweep}
\Description{Three line plots against penalty magnitude on a log axis from 1 to 500, with five agents in a shared legend beneath them. In the left panel the pink and orange lines hold near 100 percent success, the vermillion EFE line holds near 97 percent and joins them from a penalty of 100 onward, the blue Planning line steps up from about 84 percent to join EFE at a penalty of 5, and the gray dashed Myopic line stays flat near 84 percent. In the middle panel every line runs flat near plus 5 to 7 except the gray Myopic line, which falls steadily to about minus 75 at the largest penalty, and a light gray shaded strip near the top marks the reward range magnified in the third panel. The right panel repeats just that magnified range on its own axis, omitting the Myopic line, showing the blue Planning and vermillion EFE lines declining from about 7 to about 4.2 as the penalty grows, while the orange InfoGain-Tuned and pink Planning-plus-IG lines hold near 5.6 up to a penalty of about 50 and then decline with them, the pink line reaching about 4.2 at a penalty of 200, below the others there, and the orange line ending highest, near 4.6, at a penalty of 500.}
\end{figure}

Across penalties $|R^-|\in[1,500]$, EFE increases listening from $2.7$ to $5.8$ observations without reconfiguration (Figure~\ref{fig:sweep}). Re-tuning myopic InfoGain-Tuned at each penalty selects $w{=}20$ throughout, giving $4.37$ observations at every penalty. Planning+IG reaches $5.77$ observations at penalties $200$ and $500$. At low stakes, over-listening costs about $1.6$ reward relative to Planning. At the largest penalty, myopic InfoGain-Tuned nominally beats EFE ($4.61$ vs.\ $4.23$), so the sweep supports adaptation across stakes rather than uniform dominance.

\subsection{Belief Evolution}
\label{app:tw_belief}



\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_belief_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Belief evolution within one illustrative Diagnosis episode per agent, not chosen to be typical in length ($N{=}8$, $K{=}3$, accuracy 0.75). Darker = higher probability (shared colorbar). The green arrow in the left margin marks the true state row, the dashed line marks the commit point, and the three panels share the time axis so episode lengths compare directly. In the plotted episodes all three agents commit correctly, Planning after 14 observations on a belief already concentrated on the true state, EFE after 29, and InfoGain-Tuned after 32 with its darkest band on a wrong row for most of the episode.}
\label{fig:belief_heatmap}
\Description{Three heatmaps of state index, eight rows each, versus time step on a light background, where darker warm colors indicate higher belief and a shared colorbar decodes belief probability from 0 to 1. A green arrow in the left margin marks the true state row, labeled 4 true, and a dark dashed vertical line marks the commit step. The panels share the time axis. In the left panel the EFE agent's belief darkens steadily along the true state row and it commits correctly after 29 observations. In the middle panel the Planning agent's belief darkens along the true state row and it commits correctly after 14 observations, with the rest of its panel blank. In the right panel the InfoGain-Tuned agent's darkest band sits on a wrong row for most of the episode, belief concentrates on the true state only near the end, and observations continue until step 32.}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:belief_heatmap} follows illustrative Diagnosis episodes. Planning commits correctly after $14$ observations, EFE after $29$, and InfoGain-Tuned after $32$. These episodes were not selected to represent typical lengths. The battery-level comparisons appear in Section~\ref{sec:results} and Figure~\ref{fig:obs_scaling}. Tileworld's corresponding belief evolution is shown once, with all three agents, in Figure~\ref{fig:tw_comparison}.

\subsection{Exploration Efficiency}
\label{app:foraging}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{figures/fig_efficiency_curves.pdf}
\caption{Exploration efficiency on Diagnosis ($N{=}8$, $K{=}3$, 300 episodes). (a) Belief entropy decay. (b) Percentage of episodes still observing. (c) Cumulative reward. Panels are stacked on a shared time axis, shaded bands show $\pm 1$ SE across episodes, and in panel (a) a marker terminates each series at its final plotted step. Panels (a) and (c) average, at each step, only the episodes still running at that step. Planning+IG uses the myopic Info Gain agent's success-tuned weight, selected on $100$ tuning episodes and transferred to Planning+IG for this visualization, not the own-class tuning protocol of the main tables (Section~\ref{sec:methodology}). EFE stops observing later than reward-only Planning and earlier than Planning+IG, whose survival curve lies at or above EFE's at every step (panel b).}
\label{fig:efficiency}
\Description{Three stacked line plots sharing a time axis from 0 to 40, with shaded standard error bands around each curve. Top: belief entropy falls from 3 bits for all agents, where the gray Myopic series ends at a square marker by step 3, the blue line plateaus near 1.6 to 2 bits and ends near step 16, the vermillion line declines to about 1.1 bits by step 29, and the pink dotted line falls lowest, to about 0.4 bits at step 39. Middle: staircase survival curves of the percentage of episodes still observing, where the gray line drops to zero by step 3, the blue line by about step 16, the vermillion line by about step 30, and the pink line still shows over 10 percent observing at step 39. Bottom: cumulative reward declines roughly linearly for all agents, with the pink line extending furthest right and lowest, to about minus 38.}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:efficiency} averages the temporal dynamics of epistemic foraging over 300 episodes. EFE reduces entropy at a rate comparable to tuned Planning+IG but commits earlier, staying out of the diminishing-returns regime. Its survival curve falls off through steps 10 to 25 while Planning+IG's extends further right.

