\section{PyMDP Consistency Check}
\label{app:pymdp}

Wrapping \texttt{pymdp}'s standard Agent class \citep{heins2022} as an observe-then-commit agent gives $3.15$ observations, $89.8\%$ success, and $+0.480$ reward on Testbed under the canonical protocol, matching our myopic Info Gain agent at $w{=}1$ (\texttt{results\_\allowbreak summary.csv}). Our recursive $H{=}4$ EFE agent instead takes $5.50$ observations. This is a consistency check between single-step implementations, not numerical equivalence or a check of recursive EFE. It is weak evidence for the epistemic term because both single-step agents also match reward-only Planning. The implementations agree on observe-versus-commit choices at all tested uniform, intermediate, and confident two-state beliefs. Their information-gain evaluations agree qualitatively, with differences between recursive evaluation and \texttt{pymdp}'s single-step policy enumeration.

