\section{Supplementary Statistics}
\label{app:stats}

Primary comparisons use independent two-sample Welch $t$-tests on per-seed means ($n{=}5$), with Holm--Bonferroni correction. Uncorrected comparisons are marked locally. Unless stated otherwise, the family comprises all pairwise agent comparisons and reported metrics within one environment or instance: $M\binom{A}{2}$ tests for $A$ agents and $M$ metrics. Nominal sizes are $108$ for Tiger ($9$ agents, reward/success/observations), $84$ for Diagnosis and Bandit ($8$ agents), and $18$ per Inspection instance ($4$ agents, reward/accuracy/tests). Undefined zero-variance comparisons are excluded, leaving $107$ defined tests for Tiger and $83$ for Diagnosis. RockSample instead corrects within metric, $21$ tests per instance over seven agents, because pooling metrics would loosen its non-rejection-based bolding rule. The discount sweep pools its $24$ tests across environments, discounts, and metrics. The MCTS ablation corrects within metric across its three environments, and the exploration sweep within metric within each of its three comparison families (Appendix~\ref{app:pomcp}).

The six main observe-then-commit and Tileworld stats files also report pooled episode-level equal-variance tests. Substituting Welch changes no non-degenerate decision at $\alpha{=}0.05$ among their $510$ comparisons. RockSample and Inspection use Welch at both levels. Because episodes within a seed are not independent, pooled tests supplement the seed-level analysis. Bootstrap $95\%$ intervals use $10{,}000$ resamples and a fixed seed. Interpreted effect sizes are Cohen's $d$ with pooled episode-level standard deviation.

