\section{Reward-Rescaling Invariance}
\label{app:reward_scaling}

To test the scale objection directly, we multiply all rewards and costs by $k \in \{0.1, 1, 10\}$ (the factor written $\alpha$ in Proposition~\ref{prop:pi1}, renamed here because $\alpha$ also denotes reward asymmetry) on Diagnosis and Bandit and re-sweep Planning+IG weights. Maximizing $\mathbb{E}[kR] + w\,I$ induces the same policy as maximizing $\mathbb{E}[R] + (w/k)\,I$. Matching a given unscaled policy therefore requires scaling the weight up with $k$, and $w^*_{\mathrm{ret}}(k) \approx k\, w^*_{\mathrm{ret}}(1)$. Figure~\ref{fig:reward_scaling} and Table~\ref{tab:reward_scaling} report the sweep (200 episodes $\times$ 3 seeds per $(k,w)$ pair). Results confirm covariance of $w^*_{\mathrm{ret}}$ with $k$. $w{=}1$ is not scale-invariant, and the paper's near-optimality claims are conditional on the shared reward convention used throughout the suite.

