\section{Information Base and the Nat-Canonical Agent}
\label{app:nat_canonical}

The implementation measures information in bits, so the implemented $w{=}1$ agent carries an effective nat-denominated weight of $1/\ln 2 \approx 1.44$, and the exactly nat-canonical identification of Proposition~\ref{prop:equivalence} corresponds to $w = \ln 2 \approx 0.69$ in the implementation's bit units (Section~\ref{subsec:formal_rho_equiv}). The weight sweep of Section~\ref{sec:pareto} answers whether this gap matters only in part, because $0.69$ is not a grid weight. On Bandit the reward-maximizing tied run (the closed range of adjacent grid weights whose sweep rewards are exactly tied at the maximum) has grid endpoints $[1, 2]$, and the nearest swept weight below $0.69$, $w{=}0.5$, scores $6.05$ against the tied $6.24$ (\texttt{results\_\allowbreak pareto\_\allowbreak sweep.csv}). We therefore ran the exactly nat-canonical agent, $w{=}\ln 2$ in the implementation's bit units, together with a fresh $w{=}1$ run on the same seeds, under the sweep's own protocol (500 episodes $\times$ 5 seeds) on all five swept environments (\texttt{results\_\allowbreak nat\_\allowbreak canonical\_\allowbreak check.csv}, \url{experiments/run_nat_canonical_check.py}). On Tiger, Diagnosis, Bandit, and Testbed the two agents are bit-identical in reward, success rate, and mean observation count ($5.33$ and $99.6\%$ on Tiger, $-1.26$ and $97.4\%$ on Diagnosis, $6.24$ and $86.9\%$ on Bandit, $0.37$ and $96.4\%$ on Testbed), so on Bandit the nat-canonical weight lands inside the tied run rather than with $w{=}0.5$. On Testbed both agents score $0.37$ against the $0.48$ that the weights of the tied reward-maximizing run $[0.01, 0.5]$ achieve, and the separation of the two conventions by the $H{=}2$ interval is discussed after Table~\ref{tab:alpha_eta}.

Tileworld is the one swept environment where the base changes the policy. There the nat-canonical agent behaves like the sweep's $w{=}0.5$ grid point rather than like its $w{=}1$ grid point ($-20.81$ reward, $74.7\%$ success, and $15.64$ scans against $-21.53$, $72.0\%$, and $14.75$, \texttt{results\_\allowbreak nat\_\allowbreak canonical\_\allowbreak check.csv}). The difference is significant in observation count and within sampling error in reward and success rate. Seed-level Welch $t$-tests under the protocol of Appendix~\ref{app:stats} give $p{=}9.5{\times}10^{-5}$ for observation count, surviving Holm correction over the three metrics, against $p{=}0.34$ and $0.070$ for reward and success rate. The conclusion Tileworld supports is unchanged by the check. Under either unit convention $w{=}1$ is Pareto-dominated there by the larger weight $w{=}20$, which beats it on both axes of the sweep, reward and success rate ($-18.95$ and $91.8\%$, \texttt{results\_\allowbreak pareto\_\allowbreak sweep.csv}).

